## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design scalable, isolation-preserving authorization systems in streaming architectures. It covers critical cloud-native concepts including per-tenant policy governance, ABAC vs RBAC trade-offs, lightweight in-memory caches, policy versioning, and safe propagation of live policy updates without cross-tenant leakage.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy governance and attribute-based access control (ABAC)
- In-memory caching strategies with topic-scoped isolation
- Policy versioning and cryptographic signing for auditability
- Secure propagation mechanisms that prevent information leakage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo evaluator sketch
function evalPolicy(tenantId, resource, action, attrs) {
  const policy = cache.get(tenantId);
  if (!policy) return false;
  if (!policy.isCurrent()) {
    cache.refresh(tenantId); // async background refresh
    return false; // fail closed during refresh
  }
  return policy.evaluate({ resource, action, attrs });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test policy rollback and maintain audit trails?
- How would you handle policy changes during active stream processing?
- What strategies would you use to minimize cache miss latency during tenant onboarding?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses hands-on windowed aggregation, per-key state management, late-data handling, and correct offset commits in a beginner KCNA setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tumbling windows, per-key state, late data handling, TTL, idempotent emits, offset management.

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedAggSink {
  constructor(windowMs, latenessMs) {
    this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.latenessMs = latenessMs;
    this.state = new Map(); // windowStart -> deviceId -> sum
  }
  onMessage(msg) {
    // msg: { deviceId, value, timestamp, offset }
    const ws = this.windowStartFor(msg.timestamp);
    const bucket = (this.state.get(ws) = this.state.get(ws) || new Map());
    bucket.set(msg.deviceId, (bucket.get(msg.deviceId) || 0) + msg.value);
  }
  onWindowBoundary(now) {
    // emit and clean up windows that ended before now - latenessMx
  }
  commitOffsets() {
    // persist offsets after successful emits
  }
  getTotals() {
    // return current in-memory totals for inspection
  }
  windowStartFor(ts) {
    return Math.floor(ts / this.windowMs) * this.windowMs;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a stateful, TTL-based enrichment with side-input updates, late-data handling, and idempotent sinks in KCNA. Reflects real-world needs for per-user enrichment at scale.

## Key Concepts
- TTL-based in-memory caches for streaming enrichment
- Side-input stream for cache population
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in sinks
- Re-enrichment triggers and idempotent downstream writes
- Offset management across multi-topic joins

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton enrichment logic
class Enricher {
  constructor(ttlMs) {
    this.cache = new TTLCache(ttlMs); // maps userId -> segment
  }
  enrichMessage(clickEvent) {
    const seg = this.cache.get(clickEvent.userId);
    if (seg) return { ...clickEvent, segment: seg };
    // emit with unknown and schedule re-enrichment on cache update
    return { ...clickEvent, segment: 'unknown' };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test eviction and re-enrichment without data loss?
- How would you scale the TTL cache across multiple workers?
- How would you detect and recover from cache corruption or delays in side-inputs?

